description:
crate-jmap-server/src/backend.rs:199-210 — SetError::with_extra has the signature:

  pub fn with_extra(mut self, key: &str, value: serde_json::Value) -> Self {
      // ...
      self.extra.insert(key.to_owned(), value);
      self
  }

All other builder methods on the same type use `impl Into<String>`:

  pub fn with_description(mut self, desc: impl Into<String>) -> Self            // line 124
  pub fn with_properties<I, S>(mut self, props: I) -> Self where ... S: Into<String>   // line 130
  pub fn with_invalid_recipients<I, S>(...) where ... S: Into<String>            // line 152

A serious Rust practitioner reads the SetError builder API and notices the inconsistency at first call site. Callers that have a `String` (e.g. a key built from `format!`) must write `with_extra(&key, ...)` — taking a reference just so the function can clone it back into ownership via `.to_owned()`. The reference round-trip is exactly what `impl Into<String>` exists to eliminate.

What goes wrong if it stays as-is:

  1. Inconsistent builder ergonomics on a single type. Reviewers second-guess "is &str intentional here for some reason I am missing?", chase down whether the reserved-name guard requires &str (it does not — String would work fine), and conclude there is no reason.
  2. Callers with an owned String pay a `&owned_string` then `.to_owned()` copy round-trip. The cost is small, but the asymmetry is the cost the next contributor pays in attention every time they read the type.
  3. A future refactor that wants to take `Cow<str>` or borrow the key longer-term cannot, because the signature already discarded the ownership information.

Fix is a one-line signature change:

  pub fn with_extra(mut self, key: impl Into<String>, value: serde_json::Value) -> Self {
      let key: String = key.into();
      debug_assert!(
          !RESERVED_SET_ERROR_WIRE_NAMES.contains(&key.as_str()),
          // ... same message ...
      );
      self.extra.insert(key, value);
      self
  }

SemVer-safe: `impl Into<String>` accepts everything `&str` did (via `impl From<&str> for String`), so all existing call sites compile unchanged.

Low priority because the cost is small per call and the bug class is local. Logging here as a P2 because the inconsistency is a workspace-wide "canonical template" issue — `jmap-server` is the canonical *-server foundation, and the extension *-server crates will cookie-cut their own builder shapes from it. Fixing here cuts off the propagation cost.
